Case 1 is the poster child for GPU and from there it only starts going downhill.

One thing to keep in mind being even though case 1 maybe best for GPU and case 4 best for CPU,
most real world benchmarks/apps are Network of Tasks (kernels)

So even if one stage is bad and therefore a bottleneck, you may want to run it on the wrong side
to avoid communication cost. This is where a good CPU/GPU communication comes in handy of course.

So for e.g., even if you had a slow bottleneck Case 4 in the middle of a vastly Data Parallel 
Case 1, you may still want to execute the slow case on the GPU to avoid roundtrip costs. Power
follows a similar argument. 

When you execute the wrong code pattern on the wrong side you land up sucking more power.


